the location of the zaparesia plant  makes it strategically critical to both  sides it's the closest power plant to  the parts of Ukraine annexed by Russia  east of the NEPA River and to the South  Moscow needs that electricity to control  its territory on the other side Ukraine  can't afford to lose a plant capable of  supplying 20 of its electricity but from  a safety point of view this nucleus  stalemate is perilous zaparesia is the  largest nuclear power plant in Europe it  consists of six pressurized water  reactors connected to the power grid by  four lines to a high voltage substation  and one backup linking it to a nearby  coal plant more than 2 000 tons of  Highly radioactive spent nuclear fuel is  held in storage ponds and a dry store on  the site following repeated incidents  all six reactors have been shot down  grid connections to the plant have been  regularly damaged by fires linked to  mines or shelling projectiles have also  pierced buildings immediately adjacent  to the reactors and themselves  once shot down the reactors are a much  more safe state but the fuel in them and  in storage ponds still needs cooling  water if Maine's power is lost as it was  following a recent shelling incident  only backup diesel generators can pump  water to prevent a meltdown a direct hit  to a reactor or to a storage Pond could  also cause significant if localized  nuclear contamination in a worst case  scenario a loss of power plus damage to  the reactors or Safety Systems could  result in the worst nuclear accident in  more than a decade  there is hope negotiations in Moscow can  advance an end to fighting around the  plant but with neither side willing to  relinquish control the area remains an  accident waiting to happen
 

